#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "2acc65a4-62df-5e61-a3c9-cb8a08e1ff8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9831" :question/problem "![](images/07/07fcd57974679ef970ef0042fc81488b3568accb8ed22072d571f58481d0904f.svg)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n![](images/80/80628bb0c815ce0ddee2fb11ae86835a6877f26c1c38a96aea6a9394c74bcd64.svg)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](images/8e/8eaf2b04375bfefc23f6367c09259b33f02357d2015c3e68144b898491620f84.svg)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}29 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+54 = 104.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "22ff2997-c3c3-560e-afdc-52da342dcc10" :question/math-academy-id "e-9832" :question/problem "![](images/63/630c5ae74bf1c85dd7de59b9b088f8ae24095198516a1a0c0aec25be822401b4.svg)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\!A\\mathbf{x}=W\\mathbf{b},$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](images/c3/c3e1d34cc5dac7cd4d3ec91a7d71b1884a8e0694c76eb23cc16b61b144815b54.svg)\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 \\\\ -21 & 21\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-39 \\\\ 39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ -21 & 21 & 39\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}21 & -21 & -39 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{13}{7}+x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/49/49718c802fe138f06e6f2b7efa23e39e2ba59f7968422fd9a60421d69e558bb7.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5c53a9-a2e1-5c97-ae8a-eca40c100135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412635} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6fec5d-06d4-5c30-a152-268334590e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412636} {:answer/id #uuid "465218a2-69bf-5497-8469-e2312eb7efd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412637} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff085af-9e73-5865-91af-322d3998891a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412646} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9d5a16-3904-5615-8861-702cbf9dba03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9d5a16-3904-5615-8861-702cbf9dba03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412652} :answer-field/id #uuid "0676c358-31ac-5dc1-99e9-f46cd0df84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "363b8573-3582-5360-9547-0ffac7abdb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-137101" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,A=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=24+17+18=59.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c80022ef-ada2-5f00-9bb9-ed7fa254c16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381092} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed5ad28-acd3-5063-9d6c-b377e05a8327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381093} {:answer/id #uuid "259e5cef-87be-5b4f-8512-01aa74084331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381094} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd0fef6-6b5f-57ae-be36-bdf43a6ea189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381095} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7565e5-7bd3-5965-9364-1b7c26f4d2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ed5ad28-acd3-5063-9d6c-b377e05a8327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b829d612-0935-5284-96c4-0a1b6f5266a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f53e4508-f234-5830-9161-84b580e3bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-137921" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & -48 \\\\ -48 & 96\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -48 & -12 \\\\ -48 & 96 & 24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}24 & -48 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466cb1d5-bbaa-5bcf-a4b9-8e35b9769bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412638} {:answer/id #uuid "7390aa1b-1e0b-5b8f-8041-bba7a08ed6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412642} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d7ad48-ee12-53ed-ae80-3bfaa814df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412647} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0cdbb1-4af3-53da-b876-22b6640991ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412648} {:answer/id #uuid "f47c99c4-37e6-574e-a7cb-b1c519e96e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7390aa1b-1e0b-5b8f-8041-bba7a08ed6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412642} :answer-field/id #uuid "68d36053-187e-5834-b46e-6140de6fde31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e019bd1-c120-5401-ab70-85681df29503" :question/math-academy-id "q-137930" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 44 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ 27 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-81 \\\\ -243\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 & -81 \\\\ 27 & 81 & -243\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 & -81 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-9-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-9-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=-9+\\frac{(-3)}{1}=-12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b69401-b58a-50bd-bee6-020edd593b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "170" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363414} {:answer/id #uuid "c392a8b1-6f1a-5bdf-8029-e585138598b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363415} {:answer/id #uuid "fd994375-3b75-5b78-abd3-b7bee9fe2afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "219" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363416} {:answer/id #uuid "502fbb04-4565-5271-9882-7ac95ce9c4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363417} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7ecf65-e4cb-5117-9ab2-de1523e4ca5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "163" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c392a8b1-6f1a-5bdf-8029-e585138598b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363415} :answer-field/id #uuid "d94b6933-0204-5b4b-8b76-4bb96eb07292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf7e46-a2fa-5de3-bf6b-0980caaab67f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158691" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 9 \\\\ 9 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ 16 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}77 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 86\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -56\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=77+86+(-56)=107.\n$$"}]}
